"use strict";(self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith=self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith||[]).push([[53538],{553538:e=>{e.exports="# Oracle Cursors → Databricks SQL\n\nLiteral-translation rules for explicit cursors, parameterized cursors, `REF CURSOR` / `SYS_REFCURSOR` variables, `FETCH ... BULK COLLECT INTO`, and `WHERE CURRENT OF`. Split out of `04-procedures-variables.md` so the cursor patterns load on demand.\n\n> Companion files:\n>\n> - `04-procedures-variables.md` — surrounding procedure / function syntax, `SELECT ... INTO`, `SYS_REFCURSOR` **OUT-parameter** result-set return, `EXECUTE IMMEDIATE` (dynamic cursors).\n> - `04b-error-handling.md` — `DECLARE CONTINUE HANDLER FOR NOT FOUND` is the cursor-loop terminator and is documented there alongside the rest of the exception model.\n> - `02-select-dml.md` \xa7FOR UPDATE — `FOR UPDATE [OF col] [NOWAIT \\| WAIT n \\| SKIP LOCKED]` clauses on cursor declarations.\n> - `05-temp-tables-ctes.md` \xa7BULK COLLECT — full rewrite matrix when the cursor's purpose is bulk paging.\n\n## ORACLE\n\n### Cursors (literal translation)\n\n**Prefer set-based rewrites** (see `oracle/02-select-dml.md`). Use literal translation only when row-by-row semantics are strictly required. Cursor syntax is supported on **SQL warehouses** (both classic and serverless SQL warehouse types). Not supported in notebooks or jobs running on classic or serverless compute clusters.\n\n```sql\n-- Oracle PL/SQL explicit cursor\nDECLARE\n    CURSOR c_orders IS SELECT order_id, amount FROM orders WHERE status = 'pending';\n    v_id NUMBER;\n    v_amt NUMBER;\nBEGIN\n    OPEN c_orders;\n    LOOP\n        FETCH c_orders INTO v_id, v_amt;\n        EXIT WHEN c_orders%NOTFOUND;\n        UPDATE orders SET status = 'processing' WHERE order_id = v_id;\n    END LOOP;\n    CLOSE c_orders;\nEND;\n/\n\n-- DBSQL cursor syntax (SQL warehouse)\nBEGIN\n    DECLARE v_id INT;\n    DECLARE v_amt DECIMAL(18, 2);\n    DECLARE done INT DEFAULT 0;\n    DECLARE c_orders CURSOR FOR\n        SELECT order_id, amount FROM my_catalog.my_schema.orders WHERE status = 'pending';\n    DECLARE CONTINUE HANDLER FOR NOT FOUND SET done = 1;\n\n    OPEN c_orders;\n    fetch_loop: LOOP\n        FETCH c_orders INTO v_id, v_amt;\n        IF done = 1 THEN LEAVE fetch_loop; END IF;\n        UPDATE my_catalog.my_schema.orders SET status = 'processing' WHERE order_id = v_id;\n    END LOOP fetch_loop;\n    CLOSE c_orders;\nEND;\n```\n\n**`FOR rec IN (SELECT ...)` loop** — Oracle's implicit cursor loop translates the same way; unroll into explicit `OPEN`/`FETCH`/`CLOSE`.\n\n**Cursor attributes mapping:**\n\n| Oracle         | DBSQL equivalent                                              |\n| -------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `cur%FOUND`    | Inverse of the `NOT FOUND` handler flag (`done = 0`)          |\n| `cur%NOTFOUND` | The `NOT FOUND` handler flag (`done = 1`)                     |\n| `cur%ROWCOUNT` | Maintain a counter variable manually — no built-in equivalent |\n| `cur%ISOPEN`   | Track manually — no built-in equivalent                       |\n\n**`SELECT ... INTO` (implicit cursor)** — Oracle uses `SELECT col INTO var FROM t WHERE ...` for single-row retrieval. Translates to DBSQL `SET`:\n\n```sql\n-- Oracle\nSELECT name INTO v_name FROM users WHERE id = 1;\n\n-- DBSQL\nSET v_name = (SELECT name FROM my_catalog.my_schema.users WHERE id = 1);\n```\n\nOracle raises `NO_DATA_FOUND` on 0 rows and `TOO_MANY_ROWS` on >1 rows. DBSQL raises an error on >1 rows; on 0 rows the variable retains its default. If the source handled `NO_DATA_FOUND` explicitly, add a `DECLARE HANDLER` that sets the variable to a sentinel value, or use `LIMIT 1` with `ORDER BY` plus an explicit null check.\n\n#### Parameterized cursors\n\nOracle cursors can take parameters that are bound when the cursor is opened (`OPEN c(42)`). DBSQL cursors **do not accept parameters at OPEN time** — bind the values into the cursor's underlying `SELECT` via local variables that are in scope when the cursor is declared:\n\n```sql\n-- Oracle\nDECLARE\n    CURSOR c_orders(p_status VARCHAR2, p_min_amount NUMBER) IS\n        SELECT order_id, amount FROM orders\n        WHERE status = p_status AND amount >= p_min_amount;\n    v_id NUMBER;\n    v_amt NUMBER;\nBEGIN\n    OPEN c_orders('pending', 100);\n    LOOP\n        FETCH c_orders INTO v_id, v_amt;\n        EXIT WHEN c_orders%NOTFOUND;\n    END LOOP;\n    CLOSE c_orders;\nEND;\n/\n\n-- DBSQL — declare the parameters as variables in the same scope; the cursor SELECT closes over them\nBEGIN\n    DECLARE p_status STRING DEFAULT 'pending';\n    DECLARE p_min_amount DECIMAL(18, 2) DEFAULT 100;\n    DECLARE v_id INT;\n    DECLARE v_amt DECIMAL(18, 2);\n    DECLARE done INT DEFAULT 0;\n    DECLARE c_orders CURSOR FOR\n        SELECT order_id, amount FROM my_catalog.my_schema.orders\n        WHERE status = p_status AND amount >= p_min_amount;\n    DECLARE CONTINUE HANDLER FOR NOT FOUND SET done = 1;\n\n    OPEN c_orders;\n    fetch_loop: LOOP\n        FETCH c_orders INTO v_id, v_amt;\n        IF done = 1 THEN LEAVE fetch_loop; END IF;\n    END LOOP fetch_loop;\n    CLOSE c_orders;\nEND;\n```\n\nIf the same cursor is opened multiple times with different parameter values, DBSQL has **no equivalent of re-OPEN with new parameters** — declare a separate cursor per call site, or refactor to a dynamic cursor (see `04-procedures-variables.md` \xa7EXECUTE IMMEDIATE).\n\n#### REF CURSOR variables (strong vs. weak)\n\nOracle has two `REF CURSOR` flavors:\n\n- **Strong REF CURSOR**: `TYPE my_ref IS REF CURSOR RETURN orders%ROWTYPE;` — the row shape is declared at type-definition time and enforced at compile time.\n- **Weak REF CURSOR (`SYS_REFCURSOR`)**: any row shape, resolved at runtime.\n\nBoth are used as in-procedure variables that point at a cursor result set. DBSQL has **no cursor variable type at all** — there is no concept of a procedural value that holds an unfetched result set. Translation strategy:\n\n- If the REF CURSOR is **opened, then fetched, then closed inside one procedure** — translate to a regular `DECLARE c CURSOR FOR ...` and operate on the cursor directly. The variable indirection disappears.\n- If the REF CURSOR is **passed between procedures** (one opens, another fetches) — refactor so a single procedure does both, or stage the result set in a temp view that the second procedure reads. Cursor handles cannot cross procedure boundaries.\n- If the REF CURSOR is **returned to the caller as `OUT SYS_REFCURSOR`** — see `04-procedures-variables.md` \xa7REF CURSOR / SYS_REFCURSOR result sets (becomes the final `SELECT` of the procedure body).\n- If the REF CURSOR is **opened dynamically** (`OPEN v_ref FOR <SQL string> USING ...`) — see the `OPEN ... FOR <dynamic SQL>` subsection under `04-procedures-variables.md` \xa7EXECUTE IMMEDIATE.\n\nThe strong-vs-weak distinction has no DBSQL counterpart and can be ignored once the cursor variable is removed.\n\n#### `FETCH ... BULK COLLECT INTO [LIMIT n]`\n\n`FETCH cursor BULK COLLECT INTO collection [LIMIT n]` reads a batch of rows out of an open cursor in one round-trip. Common Oracle pattern is paginate-through-cursor with `LIMIT 1000`. **DBSQL has no BULK COLLECT** — see `05-temp-tables-ctes.md` \xa7BULK COLLECT for the full rewrite matrix. Two reductions:\n\n- If the loop body is set-based DML, drop the cursor + bulk pagination entirely and emit a single `INSERT … SELECT` / `MERGE` against the cursor's underlying query.\n- If the loop body genuinely needs row-by-row work, drop the BULK COLLECT and use the literal cursor pattern above (`DECLARE c CURSOR FOR ...; FETCH c INTO v1, v2;`) — DBSQL fetches one row at a time, but the procedural overhead is rarely the bottleneck after the rewrite.\n\n#### `WHERE CURRENT OF cursor`\n\nOracle's `UPDATE … WHERE CURRENT OF c` and `DELETE … WHERE CURRENT OF c` modify the row most recently fetched by cursor `c` without re-querying. **DBSQL has no `WHERE CURRENT OF`** — re-issue the predicate against a unique-key column captured during the FETCH:\n\n```sql\n-- Oracle\nDECLARE\n    CURSOR c_orders IS SELECT order_id, status FROM orders FOR UPDATE OF status;\nBEGIN\n    FOR rec IN c_orders LOOP\n        UPDATE orders SET status = 'done' WHERE CURRENT OF c_orders;\n    END LOOP;\nEND;\n/\n\n-- DBSQL — no WHERE CURRENT OF; predicate on the key\nBEGIN\n    DECLARE v_id INT;\n    DECLARE v_status STRING;\n    DECLARE done INT DEFAULT 0;\n    DECLARE c_orders CURSOR FOR\n        SELECT order_id, status FROM my_catalog.my_schema.orders;\n    DECLARE CONTINUE HANDLER FOR NOT FOUND SET done = 1;\n\n    OPEN c_orders;\n    fetch_loop: LOOP\n        FETCH c_orders INTO v_id, v_status;\n        IF done = 1 THEN LEAVE fetch_loop; END IF;\n        UPDATE my_catalog.my_schema.orders SET status = 'done' WHERE order_id = v_id;\n    END LOOP fetch_loop;\n    CLOSE c_orders;\nEND;\n\n-- DBSQL — better: drop the cursor entirely\nUPDATE my_catalog.my_schema.orders SET status = 'done';\n```\n\nIf the cursor's underlying query has no unique key (`SELECT * FROM t WHERE …`), the cursor pattern is not migratable as-is — the row identity is lost. Add a synthetic `ROW_NUMBER()` column at the cursor level and predicate the UPDATE on `(<source key columns>) = (<captured values>)`, or refactor to set-based DML.\n\nThe `FOR UPDATE OF` clause attached to the cursor declaration goes away too — see `02-select-dml.md` \xa7FOR UPDATE.\n"}}]);
